\ 1z truthiness: only `f` is falsy. The compiled `if` truthiness check reads a
\ Value's payload to test for boolean-false. It must read that payload as an
\ integer, never as a Zig bool. A non-boolean value whose low payload byte is
\ >= 2 would otherwise trip a bool load-safety panic, because the IR AND with
\ the tag check does not short-circuit the payload load.
\
\ `#len` yields a runtime fixnum (5) the compiler does not constant-fold, so the
\ `if` reads the physical slot through the truthiness path rather than folding.

len-of: ( s -- n ) [ #len ] ;

"abcde" len-of [ "truthy" ] [ "falsy" ] if print-line  \ 5 is truthy
